            <ArticleTitle>Revision THA in obese patients is associated with high re-operation rates at short-term follow-up.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>209-13</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.arth.2014.03.046</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0883-5403(14)00331-3</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We performed a retrospective review of 309 consecutive revision THAs from 2005 to 2009. We identified a subgroup of patients with BMI &gt;35 and compared the operative time, rate of complications, ICU admissions, re-admissions, and re-operations to patients with BMI &lt;35 undergoing revision THA. At a mean follow-up of 36.3 months, there was no significant difference in operative time, perioperative complications, or re-admission rate between the two groups. However, a significantly higher rate of re-operation was observed in the obese group (46% vs. 28%, P=0.015). Obese patients were more likely to undergo reoperation for infection (P=0.017). Patients with high BMI contemplating primary THA should be aware of the potential subsequent complications associated with revision surgery should it become necessary.</AbstractText>
